<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yodo-dono</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1569">1569</a>–<a href="/years/1615">1615</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Yodogimi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yodo-dono, born <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-felix-de-souza">Chacha</a>, was the eldest daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azai-nagamasa">Azai Nagamasa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oichi">Oichi</a> and became a consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>. She bore his heir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> Hideyori, and after Hideyoshi's death led the Toyotomi faction at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a>. She died with her son when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> in 1615.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yodo-dono.jpg" alt="Yodo-dono" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 奈良県立美術館収蔵『傳 淀殿畫像』 / Nara Museum of Art · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yodo-dono_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
